  23:     Complete the sparse colored marker pattern by reflecting it across a full-row/full-column cross.
  24: 
  25:     Input structure
  26:     The grid has a dominant background and one non-background cross color that
  27:     fills exactly one complete row and one complete column. All other foreground
  28:     pixels are sparse markers away from that cross.
  29: 
  30:     Rule
  31:     Keep the input unchanged, and for each marker pixel copy its color to the
  32:     positions obtained by reflecting it across the cross row, across the cross
  33:     column, and across both axes. Reflected positions outside the grid are
  34:     clipped.
  35: 
  36:     Key insight
  37:     The full cross is not a copied object; it is the pair of mirror axes for
  38:     completing the quadrant pattern.
  39:     """
  40:     g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)
  41:     bg = get_bg(g)
  42:     axis_color, axis_r, axis_c = _find_cross_axes(g, bg)
  43: 
  44:     marker_coords = np.argwhere((g != bg) & (g != axis_color))
  45:     assert marker_coords.size > 0, "expected sparse markers off the cross"
  46: 
  47:     out = g.copy()
  48:     for r0, c0 in marker_coords:
  49:         r = int(r0)
  50:         c = int(c0)
  51:         color = int(g[r, c])
  52:         assert r != axis_r and c != axis_c, "marker pixels must not lie on the cross axes"
  53: 
  54:         for rr in (r, 2 * axis_r - r):
  55:             for cc in (c, 2 * axis_c - c):
  56:                 if not in_bounds(g.shape, rr, cc):
  57:                     continue
  58:                 existing = int(out[rr, cc])
  59:                 assert existing in (bg, color), "reflected marker pixels must not conflict"
  60:                 out[rr, cc] = color
  61: 
  62:     return out
  63: 
  64: # --------------- generator (att2) ---------------
